Kernir patch 0018: the xar reader decodes a base64 <name> once, and joins the pieces of an <ea>'s <name>

Upstream: libarchive 3.8.10, libarchive/archive_read_support_format_xar.c (struct xar, xar_cleanup, xml_start,
xml_end, xml_data, the expat, libxml2 and xmllite callbacks that call xml_end). Follows up
https://github.com/libarchive/libarchive/issues/3849. Applies on top of 0001-0017 (0015, 0016 and 0017 lines
included).

Patch 0017 joined the pieces expat hands over for one <name> (split at entity and character references, newlines
and the end of each 64 KiB chunk of inflated TOC), but a <name enctype="base64"> was still decoded piece by piece
by strappend_base64, so a piece that ended inside a 4-character group decoded wrongly: "QUJ" + "D" read "AB",
"QU" + "JD" read garbage. Base64 wrapped into lines that are not whole groups, a name across a chunk boundary and
one written with character references all hit this. And an <ea>'s <name> (FILE_EA_NAME) was archive_strncpy'd,
so it kept only its last piece: <ea><name>x&amp;y</name></ea> named the attribute "y".

xml_data now collects the raw text of a base64 <name> (not the ignored second <name> of 0017) in
kernir_name_b64text, leaving out whitespace (space, tab, CR, LF: base64 may be wrapped anywhere) and refusing more
than KERNIR_XAR_NAME_B64_CHARS = 87,384 characters, 65,536 bytes' worth (the per-name message of 0015), so the text
held before a refusal stays bounded. xml_end decodes it once at </name> (kernir_xar_name_b64_end), adds the parent
prefix and sets HAS_PATHNAME as xml_data does for a plain name, and charges 0015's per-name limit (prefix
included) and 0016's names total on the decoded name, once, after the per-name check. xml_end therefore returns an
int now and the three XML callbacks pass a refusal on. Whether a <name> is base64 is fixed when it starts
(kernir_name_b64), so an element nested in it cannot switch its later pieces to plain text. Decoding still stops
at the first character outside the base64 alphabet ("=" included), as strappend_base64 did per piece.

An <ea>'s <name> now joins its pieces: the first piece of each <name> empties the attribute name (the last <name>
counts, whole, and an empty <name/> leaves the earlier one, as upstream) and the joined name is refused over
65,536 bytes (the per-name message of 0015). It is a separate string with its own state (kernir_ea_name_started),
so neither name can spill into the other.

Apply from Sources/CLibArchive: patch -p1 < patches/0018-xar-name-decode-once.patch

